x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_1x + 66 \\) with integer coefficients and distinct integer roots, we need to find the least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\).\n\nLet the roots of the polynomial be \\( r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n \\). Then, the polynomial can be expressed as:\n\\[\nf(x) = 2(x - r_1)(x - r_2) \\cdots (x - r_n)\n\\]\nExpanding this product, we get:\n\\[\nf(x) = 2 \\left( x^n - (r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n)x^{n-1} + \\cdots + (-1)^n r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n \\right)\n\\]\nGiven that \\( a_0 = 66 \\), we have:\n\\[\n2(-1)^n r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n = 66\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n(-1)^n r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n = 33\n\\]\n\nThe integer factorizations of 33 are:\n\\[\n33 = 3 \\times 11, \\quad 33 = -3 \\times -11, \\quad 33 = 1 \\times 33, \\quad 33 = -1 \\times -33\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the roots \\( r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n \\) such that their product is 33 (or -33) and the sum \\( r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n \\) is minimized in absolute value. We will consider the factorizations and their sums:\n\n1. \\( 33 = 3 \\times 11 \\)\n   \\[\n   r_1 = 3, \\quad r_2 = 11 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad r_1 + r_2 = 14\n   \\]\n\n2. \\( 33 = -3 \\times -11 \\)\n   \\[\n   r_1 = -3, \\quad r_2 = -11 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad r_1 + r_2 = -14\n   \\]\n\n3. \\( 33 = 1 \\times 33 \\)\n   \\[\n   r_1 = 1, \\quad r_2 = 33 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad r_1 + r_2 = 34\n   \\]\n\n4. \\( 33 = -1 \\times -33 \\)\n   \\[\n   r_1 = -1, \\quad r_2 = -33 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad r_1 + r_2 = -34\n   \\]\n\nAmong these, the smallest absolute value of the sum is \\( 14 \\) (or \\(-14\\)). Therefore, the least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) is:\n\\[\n|a_{n-1}| = 2 \\times 14 = 28\n\\]\n\nThus, the least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{28}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{28}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
